def bubble_sort(arr):
    n = len(arr)

    for i in range(n):
        # Last i elements are already in place
        for j in range(0, n-i-1):
            # Traverse the array from 0 to n-i-1
            # Swap if the element found is greater
            # than the next element
            if arr[j] > arr[j+1]:
                arr[j], arr[j+1] = arr[j+1], arr[j]
    return arr

def main():
    unsorted_arr = [64, 34, 25, 12, 22, 11, 90]
    sorted_arr = bubble_sort(unsorted_arr)
    print("Sorted array is:", sorted_arr)

if __name__ == "__main__":
    main()
```

In this solution, the `bubble_sort` function is implemented from scratch with a time complexity of O(n^2) in the worst-case scenario and O(n^2) in the best-case scenario due to the nested loops. The function iterates through the list and compares adjacent elements, swapping them if they are in the wrong order. This process is repeated until the entire list is sorted. The `main` function demonstrates the usage of `bubble_sort` by sorting a sample array and printing the sorted array.

